.TH MU2EDAQ-POWER-RECOVERY-BOOTSTRAP 1 "2026-09-17" "mu2edaq-power-recovery 0.1.0" "Mu2e DAQ Operations"
.SH NAME
bootstrap.sh, bootstrap.ps1, start-mu2edaq-power-recovery.sh, stop-mu2edaq-power-recovery.sh \- set up and control a recovery run
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ./bootstrap.sh
.RB [ \-\-with\-cpp ]
.br
.B ./bootstrap.ps1
.RB [ \-WithCpp ]
.br
.B ./start-mu2edaq-power-recovery.sh
.RI [ options ...]
.br
.B ./stop-mu2edaq-power-recovery.sh
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.RB [ \-\-status ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.SS bootstrap.sh, bootstrap.ps1
Creates or refreshes the project's Python virtual environment in
.IR venv/ ,
installs the package in editable mode with its dependencies, regenerates
.I requirements.txt
from what was actually resolved, and creates the
.IR logs/ ", " data/ ", " html/ " and " core/
directories.
.PP
It is idempotent and quick when there is nothing to do, which matters because
phase 0 of
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1)
calls it whenever a self-update touched
.IR pyproject.toml ,
.I requirements.txt
or the C++ sources.
.PP
.B \-\-with\-cpp
additionally configures and builds
.BR libmu2eprobe (3)
with CMake. That library is optional; without it the tools use a pure-Python
reachability fallback.
.PP
.B MU2EDAQ_PYTHON
selects the interpreter (default
.BR python3 );
Python 3.9 is the minimum.
.B VENV_DIR
relocates the environment.
.SS start-mu2edaq-power-recovery.sh
Bootstraps the environment if it is missing, then passes every argument
straight to
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1).
This is the entry point to use from a fresh clone, because it works before the
venv exists.
.PP
With no arguments it runs
.B \-\-phase assess
\(em the read-only survey \(em on the principle that an accidental bare
invocation should look at the cluster rather than change it.
.PP
It writes a pid file to
.I logs/power-recovery.pid
and refuses to start a second concurrent run, which would drive the same
cluster from two directions and interleave power commands.
.SS stop-mu2edaq-power-recovery.sh
Stops a running recovery. SIGTERM first: the driver catches it, records the
interruption in the run store and closes the phase it is in, so the report
still reflects what was actually done. SIGKILL only after a grace period
.RB ( GRACE ,
default 30 seconds), and only if it is needed.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Kill immediately. The run store may not record the interruption.
.TP
.B \-\-status
Report whether a run is active and change nothing.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I venv/
The virtual environment.
.TP
.I logs/power-recovery.pid
Pid of the active run.
.TP
.I requirements.txt
Regenerated by
.BR bootstrap.sh .
Edit
.I pyproject.toml
instead.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.EX
\&./bootstrap.sh
\&./bootstrap.sh \-\-with\-cpp
MU2EDAQ_PYTHON=python3.9 ./bootstrap.sh

\&./start-mu2edaq-power-recovery.sh                       # survey only
\&./start-mu2edaq-power-recovery.sh \-\-phase all \-\-simulate
\&./stop-mu2edaq-power-recovery.sh \-\-status
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1),
.BR mu2edaq-power-recovery (7)
.SH AUTHOR
A. Norman <anorman@fnal.gov>, Mu2e DAQ Operations.
